¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙbAnd unto the married I command, yet not I, but the Lord, Let not the wife depart from her husband:¦layout ªlast_verse
„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ|but and if she depart, let her remain unmarried, or be reconciled to her husband: and let not the husband put away his wife.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book-§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ•But to the rest speak I, not the Lord: If any brother hath a wife that believeth not, and she be pleased to dwell with him, let him not put her away.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙvAnd the woman which hath an husband that believeth not, and if he be pleased to dwell with her, let her not leave him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¥For the unbelieving husband is sanctified by the wife, and the unbelieving wife is sanctified by the husband: else were your children unclean; but now are they holy.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‰But if the unbelieving depart, let him depart. A brother or a sister is not under bondage in such cases: but God hath called us to peace.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFor what knowest thou, O wife, whether thou shalt save thy husband? or how knowest thou, O man, whether thou shalt save thy wife?¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book-§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses˜„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ~But as God hath distributed to every man, as the Lord hath called every one, so let him walk. And so ordain I in all churches.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙƒIs any man called being circumcised? let him not become uncircumcised. Is any called in uncircumcision? let him not be circumcised.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙcCircumcision is nothing, and uncircumcision is nothing, but the keeping of the commandments of God.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ>Let every man abide in the same calling wherein he was called.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙaArt thou called being a servant? care not for it: but if thou mayest be made free, use it rather.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙŒFor he that is called in the Lord, being a servant, is the Lord's freeman: likewise also he that is called, being free, is Christ's servant.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ:Ye are bought with a price; be not ye the servants of men.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFBrethren, let every man, wherein he is called, therein abide with God.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book-§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙNow concerning virgins I have no commandment of the Lord: yet I give my judgment, as one that hath obtained mercy of the Lord to be faithful.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙjI suppose therefore that this is good for the present distress, I say, that it is good for a man so to be.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ`Art thou bound unto a wife? seek not to be loosed. Art thou loosed from a wife? seek not a wife.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙœBut and if thou marry, thou hast not sinned; and if a virgin marry, she hath not sinned. Nevertheless such shall have trouble in the flesh: but I spare you.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book-§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙuBut this I say, brethren, the time is short: it remaineth, that both they that have wives be as though they had none;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‘and they that weep, as though they wept not; and they that rejoice, as though they rejoiced not; and they that buy, as though they possessed not;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ\and they that use this world, as not abusing it: for the fashion of this world passeth away.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book-§chapter«first_verse ªlast_verse#¦verses”„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‰But I would have you without carefulness. He that is unmarried careth for the things that belong to the Lord, how he may please the Lord:¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ_but he that is married careth for the things that are of the world, how he may please his wife.¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙùThere is difference also between a wife and a virgin. The unmarried woman careth for the things of the Lord, that she may be holy both in body and in spirit: but she that is married careth for the things of the world, how she may please her husband.¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ£And this I speak for your own profit; not that I may cast a snare upon you, but for that which is comely, and that ye may attend upon the Lord without distraction.¦layout ªlast_verse#…¤book-§chapter«first_verse$ªlast_verse&¦verses“„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙºBut if any man think that he behaveth himself uncomely toward his virgin, if she pass the flower of her age, and need so require, let him do what he will, he sinneth not: let them marry.¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¶Nevertheless he that standeth stedfast in his heart, having no necessity, but hath power over his own will, and hath so decreed in his heart that he will keep his virgin, doeth well.¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙgSo then he that giveth her in marriage doeth well; but he that giveth her not in marriage doeth better.¦layout ªlast_verse&…¤book-§chapter«first_verse'ªlast_verse(¦verses’„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ›The wife is bound by the law as long as her husband liveth; but if her husband be dead, she is at liberty to be married to whom she will; only in the Lord.¦layout ªlast_verse'„¥verse(¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙfBut she is happier if she so abide, after my judgment: and I think also that I have the Spirit of God.¦layout ªlast_verse(